# What is in this endpoint? The classes a sweep sampled from it, how many it
# found, and whether that list was cut short.
#
# ?endpoint is the input. It is substituted with a NamedNode before execution
# (SEP-0007 substitution, which is why it appears in the SELECT projection:
# pyoxigraph only substitutes projected variables), so it is never
# interpolated into this text.
#
# One row per sampled class. The run, its timestamp, the size and the
# truncation flag repeat on every row because they all describe the single
# sample those classes came from. No rows at all means no run in the store
# sampled this endpoint's classes, which is a different answer from a sweep
# that sampled and found nothing: see web/endpoint_content.py.
#
# A sample is tied to its run by the graph it lives in, not by
# prov:wasGeneratedBy, because a sample is not obliged to carry that link
# (web/tests/fixtures/run-truncated.nq has none) whereas every quad the
# prober writes lands in its run's graph by construction.
#
# "Most recent" is decided by the activity's prov:generatedAtTime, a typed
# xsd:dateTime, and NOT by ordering the run IRI as a string. Today's run IRIs
# happen to embed an ISO-8601 instant, so string ordering would agree with
# this on every fixture we have and would quietly start returning a stale run
# the day that IRI shape changes. Ordering by the timestamp the data actually
# models has no such trapdoor.
#
# It is expressed as "no newer run sampled this same endpoint" rather than as
# an ORDER BY ... LIMIT 1 subquery. Both read the same at first glance, and
# the subquery form is wrong: the value substituted for ?endpoint does not
# reach inside a subquery's own projection, so the subquery would pick the
# newest run in the whole store and then fail to join whenever that run did
# not happen to sample the endpoint being asked about. NOT EXISTS is
# evaluated against the current solution, so ?endpoint is bound inside it and
# the comparison stays per-endpoint.

PREFIX prov: <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#>
PREFIX sw: <urn:sparqlwatch:>

SELECT ?endpoint ?run ?generatedAt ?size ?truncated ?class
WHERE {
  GRAPH ?run {
    ?sample sw:sampledFrom ?endpoint ;
            sw:sampledBy sw:metric:classes ;
            sw:sampleSize ?size ;
            sw:sampleTruncated ?truncated .
    ?activity a prov:Activity ;
              prov:generatedAtTime ?generatedAt .
    # OPTIONAL so a sample that genuinely found no classes still reports its
    # size and truncation flag instead of vanishing, which the caller could
    # not tell apart from never having been sampled at all.
    OPTIONAL { ?sample sw:sampledValue ?class }
  }
  FILTER NOT EXISTS {
    GRAPH ?newerRun {
      ?newerSample sw:sampledFrom ?endpoint ;
                   sw:sampledBy sw:metric:classes .
      ?newerActivity a prov:Activity ;
                     prov:generatedAtTime ?newerGeneratedAt .
    }
    FILTER (?newerGeneratedAt > ?generatedAt)
  }
}
